{"causes":["By 350 BCE, Athens had become a prominent city-state, with significant economic and cultural ties throughout the Greek world.","The rise of Athenian democracy was largely driven by figures such as Pericles and Cleisthenes who laid the groundwork for Aeschines' own success.","Aeschines' family connections allowed him to secure key roles in the Athenian government"],"effects":["The development of Western legal precedents owes a significant debt to Aeschines' pioneering work in prosecuting politicians.","Aeschines' involvement with Timocles helped shape the course of Athenian politics for generations.","His conviction and exile led to increased scrutiny of political opponents, contributing to the erosion of democratic institutions in Athens."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"demosthenes","sourceName":"Demosthenes","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"aeschines","targetName":"Aeschines","context":"Both orators were prominent members of the Athenian elite and frequently appeared together in public debates."},{"sourceSlug":"timocles","sourceName":"Timocles","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"aeschines","targetName":"Aeschines","context":"Aeschines was prosecuted by Demosthenes for his alleged ties to Timocles."},{"sourceSlug":"athens","sourceName":"Athens","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"aeschines","targetName":"Aeschines","context":"As a member of the Athenian elite, Aeschines played a significant role in shaping the city-state's politics."},{"sourceSlug":"greece","sourceName":"Greece","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"aeschines","targetName":"Aeschines","context":"Aeschines' rise to prominence coincided with the height of Greek cultural and economic influence."},{"sourceSlug":"pericles","sourceName":"Pericles","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"aeschines","targetName":"Aeschines","context":"As a prominent Athenian leader, Pericles helped lay the groundwork for Aeschines' own success."},{"sourceSlug":"cleisthenes","sourceName":"Cleisthenes","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"aeschines","targetName":"Aeschines","context":"Like Pericles, Cleisthenes played a significant role in shaping Athenian democracy."},{"sourceSlug":"athenian-democracy","sourceName":"Athenian Democracy","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"aeschines","targetName":"Aeschines","context":"As one of the ten most influential Attic orators, Aeschines helped shape the development of Athenian democracy."},{"sourceSlug":"greece","sourceName":"Greece","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"aeschines","targetName":"Aeschines","context":"Aeschines' involvement with Timocles was closely tied to the broader Greek world and its cultural and economic ties."},{"sourceSlug":"timocles","sourceName":"Timocles","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"aeschines","targetName":"Aeschines","context":"The trial that led to Aeschines' downfall was a significant moment in Athenian politics."},{"sourceSlug":"athenian-politics","sourceName":"Athenian Politics","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"aeschines","targetName":"Aeschines","context":"Aeschines' involvement with Timocles contributed to the erosion of democratic institutions in Athens."}],"places":[{"name":"Athens","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Athens","role":"Major battle"},{"name":"Athens","role":"Seat of government"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Aeschines","type":"Reference article","slug":"aeschines"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q317089","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aeschines"],"tags":["Political Leaders","political-systems","geopolitical-linkage","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Aeschines (c. 389–314 BCE) was an Athenian statesman and orator — the third of the canonical 'Ten Attic Orators' in the ancient classification — and the chief rival and nemesis of Demosthenes, the greatest orator of Classical Athens. A self-made man of modest origins (his father ran a grammar school and his mother performed mystery rites), Aeschines rose through his extraordinary talent for improvised speech and his physical presence — he was a trained actor before entering politics, and his theatrical skills gave him a style of oratory that was the antithesis of Demosthenes' laboured, intensely prepared speeches. The personal and political rivalry between Aeschines and Demosthenes, conducted through two decades of lawsuits, counter-accusations, and political manoeuvres, is one of the most fully documented political conflicts of the ancient world.

Aeschines represented the pro-Macedonian or 'Philippist' faction in Athens — advocating for accommodation with Philip II of Macedon and later Alexander the Great at a time when Demosthenes was leading the anti-Macedonian resistance. His most famous trial came in 343 BCE when Demosthenes prosecuted him for misconduct during the Peace of Philocrates negotiations (346 BCE) — Aeschines had participated in the embassy to Philip that led to Athens' controversial acceptance of the peace. Aeschines turned the tables and counter-prosecuted (in 343 BCE), but the case ended without conviction. The climactic confrontation came in 330 BCE: Aeschines prosecuted the prominent Athenian Ctesiphon for proposing to award Demosthenes a golden crown for his service to Athens. This produced Demosthenes' masterpiece 'On the Crown' — the greatest speech of ancient oratory — and Aeschines' own 'Against Ctesiphon'. Aeschines lost so decisively (failing to reach even one-fifth of the jury's votes) that he was fined and forced into exile in Rhodes, where he taught rhetoric.

Only three of Aeschines' speeches survive intact: 'Against Timarchus' (345 BCE), 'On the False Embassy' (343 BCE), and 'Against Ctesiphon' (330 BCE) — primary sources for the turbulent politics of Athens in the era of Macedonian expansion.","subjects":["Classical Athens","Greek Oratory","Classical Era","Ancient Greece","Political History","Ancient History","Macedonian Expansion","Democracy"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Aeschines' involvement with Timocles and his subsequent conviction had significant consequences for Athenian politics, contributing to the erosion of democratic institutions.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
